<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold III, Duke of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1351">1351</a>–<a href="/years/1386">1386</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Leopold the Just · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg">Treaty of Neuberg</a> of 1379 he and his brother Albert III divided the family lands, Leopold taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the western territories later called Further Austria, founding the Leopoldine line. He acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, but was killed fighting the Swiss Confederates at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> in 1386.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Leopold III, Duke of Austria" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoni Boys · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_III_of_Austria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 1 November 1351, a younger son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-austria">Albert II of Austria</a> and a grandson of King Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. His mother, Joanna of Pfirt, was 51 when she gave him birth and died shortly afterwards. His eldest brother, Rudolf IV, called the Founder, took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> dominions on their father's death, despite Albert II's rules for joint rule, and on 18 November 1364 issued his own house law, the Rudolfinische Hausordnung, declaring the hereditary lands a common possession of the brothers, with extra rights to the eldest. On 23 February 1365 Leopold married Viridis Visconti, second daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and they had four sons and three daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Rudolf died on 27 July 1365, Albert III and Leopold took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, Albert holding the additional rights as eldest. While Albert ruled, Leopold became a general leading Habsburg troops. In 1368 he defeated a Bavarian incursion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, and by 1370 all of Tyrol was under Habsburg authority. In 1372 he broke with his brother over rights, prestige and income, and a peace treaty of 25 July 1373 gave him control of Tyrol, Further Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, with income split between the dukes.</p><p>By 1375 Leopold had inherited the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> possessions in the Windic March, White Carniola, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> and Istria, and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feldkirch">Feldkirch</a>. When Albert went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1377, he was left in charge of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands. On 7 July 1379 the brothers signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg">Treaty of Neuberg</a>, which made Leopold exclusive ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, Carniola, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the Further Austrian lands in Swabia. In 1382 he was granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> as part of his payment for defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leopold's further attempts to expand his position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> failed when he was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> on 9 July 1386. He was first buried at Königsfelden Monastery; his remains were moved to St Blaise Abbey in a ceremony on 14 November 1770 and finally to Saint Paul's Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. He was the head and progenitor of the Leopoldian line, and his eldest son William succeeded him. His other sons included Leopold IV, Ernest the Iron and Frederick IV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold III, Duke of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Morgarten in 1315 they ambushed a Habsburg army between a mountain slope and Lake Ägeri; at the Battle of Sempach in 1386 they defeated and killed Duke <b>Leopold III of Austria</b>, and at the Battle of Näfels in 1388 the men of Glarus routed another Habsburg force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Neuberg of 1379 divided the family's possessions, giving the western territories, together with Tyrol and Inner Austria, to <b>Leopold III</b>; these western lands became known as Further Austria.</blockquote><a href="/state/further-austria">Further Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Neuberg of 1379 gave <b>Leopold III</b> the Habsburg lands south and west of Austria, including Styria, Carinthia and Carniola.</blockquote><a href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Sempach, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1373" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leopold III, Duke of Austria"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4231 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4231 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1373">What was here in 1373? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
